<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  `F-173` — the ring the bubble is dropped into to snooze it.

  ## Why a ring and not a filled disc

  Because it is a **destination, not a control.** A filled circle the size of
  the bubble reads as a second button, and for the half-second it is on screen
  the user has to work out which of the two round things they are supposed to
  be looking at. An outlined ring with a dim centre reads as a place — the
  bubble goes *in* it — which is the whole idea being communicated and there is
  no time to communicate it in words.

  Messenger's delete target is the same shape for the same reason, and so is
  every launcher's "drop here to remove".

  ## Why the colours are hard-coded, like the bubble's

  Same rule as `swip_bubble_bg.xml` and the camera overlays: this is drawn over
  an arbitrary app that SWIP does not control and cannot measure, so it carries
  its own contrast rather than borrowing a ground it cannot see. Following the
  Paper/Foil palette here would produce an ink ring on a dark app exactly when
  somebody needed to find it.

  The fill is deliberately very low alpha. The target has to be findable
  without hiding what is behind it, because the user is mid-gesture and still
  needs to see where the bubble is going.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="oval">

    <solid android:color="#B8060507" />
    <stroke
        android:width="2dp"
        android:color="@color/swip_bubble_edge" />
</shape>
